{
	title: 'Record declared plugin API changes at commit time',
	spec: 'Hal development commits declare Plugin-API: none or explicit compatible, rename, or breaking API changes. The implementing agent classifies semantics; no signature or body-change heuristic guesses compatibility. A commit-msg hook validates declarations and records one integer API version increment per declared batch in a tracked ASON ledger, preserving existing history. Breaking records include porting instructions. The ledger is independent of Hal releases and Git commit identities and requires no runtime Git or model work. Missing or contradictory declarations fail visibly. Record generation uses staged content, preserves unstaged and unrelated work, and handles failed-commit retries without duplicate increments; message-only amendments do not create new versions. The baseline API version is 1. Document the declaration syntax and responsibilities in developer guidance and task artifacts. Keep Git integration regression tests for ledger persistence, validation, retries and staging safety. This first version records compatibility knowledge only; plugin loading, automatic adapters, project scoping and catalogs remain unchanged.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Real Git integration tests showed that commit-msg index edits are not included in the commit tree Git already built. The hook therefore stages the generated ledger and aborts with explicit review/repeat instructions; the next attempt validates the pending entry and succeeds without another increment. Explicit-path commits must include plugin-api.ason on the second attempt. This preserves the invariant that source and its API declaration land together. No compatibility inference or runtime loader changes are in this task.',
		'Verification: 5 real-Git integration tests pass, covering initial baseline, none, multi-change recording, message-only amend, invalid declarations, prepare/repeat, unstaged ledger/source preservation, append-only history and explicit-path commits with unrelated staged work. Conventions and existing commit/file-change suites add 17 passing tests. Full TypeScript noEmit and scoped oxlint pass. Runtime cost is zero: only commit hooks read the ledger, with linear parsing/writing in recorded API changes, no Git history traversal and no dependencies added.',
